VAX/VMS Virtual Memory
  - 32-bit address space with 512 bytes page size:
    - System space (upper-half):
      - Usually only used half of it (?).
      - Protected operating system code and data reside
        in system space, and those code and data shared
        across processes.
    - Process space (lower-half):
      - P0: Where the user program memory (including heap).
      - P1: Where the stack memory.

  - One major concern of the VAX/VMS operating system is that the page size
    is too small (512 bytes), making the simple linear page table too large.
    Thus, one of the first goal of the VAX/VMS operating system designers was
    to ensure that VAX/VMS operating system would not "overwhelm" memory with
    page tables.

  - VAX/VMS memory management system reduced the pressure of
    page tables on memory in 2 ways:
    - Segmenting the user address space (or process space) into 2 regions
      (P0 and P1). VAX-11 machine provides a page table for each of these
      regions per process. Thus, no page table space is needed for the unused
      portion of the address space _between_ stack and heap.
    - The operating system reduces memory pressure even further by placing user
      page tables (P0 and P1, 2 page tables per process) in kernel virtual
      address space. Thus, when allocating or growing the user page tables, the
      kernel allocates space out of its own virtual address space in system
      space segment.

  - Page table entry (PTE) in VAX/VMS operating system contains:
    - A valid bit.
    - A protection bits (4 bits).
    - A modified (or dirty) bit.
    - A bits reserved for operating system use (5 bits).
    - A physical frame number (PFN).

  - Segmented FIFO (page replacement policy in VAX/VMS operating system):
    - Each process has a maximum number of pages it can keep in memory, known as
      "Resident Set Size" (RSS). Each of these pages is kept on a FIFO (First In
      First Out) list, and when a process exceeds its RSS, the "first-in" page
      is taken out.
    - To improve the FIFO's performance, VAX/VMS operating system introduced two
      second-chance list where pages are placed before getting evicted or taken
      out from memory, specifically a global "clean-page list" and
      "dirty page list". When a process exceeds its RSS, a page is removed
      from the FIFO list (the main memory list). And if those page is not
      modified (a.k.a clean), it is placed on the end of the "clean-page list",
      and if those page is modified or having some data in it (a.k.a dirty), it
      is placed on the end of the "dirty-page list".
    - If another process need a free page, that process can take the first free
      page from the global "clean-page list". However, if the previous
      process need those page before it is reclaimed by another process, the
      previous process can reclaim it from the "clean-page list" or
      "dirty-page list" and avoid disk access because of swapping the memory to
      disk.
    - The bigger these global second-chance list is, the closer the segmented
      FIFO policy perform to LRU (Least Recently Used) policy.

  - Swapping large batches of pages to disk:
    - Because disk I/O is slow, swapping those small page to disk one by one can
      be inefficient as "disks perform better with large transfers".
    - To make swapping more efficient, one of the technique that VAX/VMS
      operating system do is "clustering". With clustering, the operating
      system grouping a large batch of pages together from the global
      "dirty-page list", and write them to disk all at once, and thus making
      those pages "clean".

  - Demand zeroing (lazy memory allocations):
    - For example, let's say we want to allocate heap memory to our application.
      The naive implementation is that:
      - The operating system respond to our heap memory allocation request by
        finding a page in physical memory.
      - Zeroing those page (for security).
      - Mapping that physical page into our application's address space (setting
        up the page table entry).
    - With demand zeroing, the operating system put a page table entry in our
      application's address space and mark those page as inaccessible. If the
      process then read or write to the page, a trap will be triggered (page
      fault) and operating system take over the process. The operating system
      will notice that this page is a "demand-zero" page (usually from bit flag
      in "reserved for operating system" bits), and then the operating system
      start finding the page in physical memory, zeroing that page, and mapping
      it into the process' address space. After that, the operating system
      passing the control back to the application.
    - If the process never access those "demand-zero" page, the operating system
      avoid all of that tasks.

  - Copy-on-Write (CoW):
    - When the operating system need to copy a page from one address space to
      another, the operating system map that page into the target address space
      and mark the page as read-only in "both address spaces".
    - If both of the processes only read the "copied" page, no further action is
      taken. However, if one of the processes trying to write to those "copied"
      page, a trap will be triggered and operating system take over the process.
      The operating system will notice that the page is CoW page, and thus start
      allocating new page, fill it with data, and map this new page into the
      address space of process that triggered a trap. After that, the operating
      system passing the control back to the application and the process
      continue with its own private copy of the page.
    - One of the benefit of using CoW approach is the shared library. The shared
      library can be mapped into the address space of many processes, saving
      valuable memory space (is this related to linking process with linkers?).

Linux Virtual Memory (x86)
  - Linux kernel address space:
    - In classic 32-bit Linux kernel (Linux kernel with 32-bit virtual address
      space), the split between user applications and kernel portions of the
      address space takes place at address 0xC0000000, or 3:1 of address space.
      Thus, the virtual addresses from 0 until 0xBFFFFFFF are for user
      applications (3 GB), and virtual addresses from 0xC0000000 until
      0xFFFFFFFF are for kernel (1 GB). 64-bit Linux kernel has a similar split
      but at slightly different points.
    - Linux kernel contains 2 types of virtual addresses:
      - Kernel logical address:
        - This is the normal virtual address space of the kernel. To get more
          memory in this address space, we can use kmalloc().
        - Most kernel data structures live here, such as page tables,
          per-process kernel stacks, and so forth.
        - Kernel logical address cannot be swapped to disk.
        - There's a _direct_ mapping between logical addresses and the first
          portion of physical memory. Thus, kernel logical address 0xC0000000
          translates to physical address 0x00000000, 0xC0000FFF translates to
          physical address 0x00000FFF, and so on. This direct mapping has 2
          implications:
          - Simple to translate. These addresses are often treated as if they
            are indeed physical.
          - If a chunk of memory is contiguous in kernel logical address space,
            it is also contiguous in physical memory. This is suitable for
            operations which need contiguous physical memory to work correctly,
            such as I/O transfers to and from devices via direct memory access
            (DMA).
      - Kernel virtual address:
        - To get more memory in this address space, we can use vmalloc() which
          returns a pointer to a "virtually" contiguous region of the specified
          size.
        - Kernel virtual address may map to non-contiguous physical pages (and
          is not suitable for DMA), which make allocate large buffer easier
          compared to kernel logical address.
        - In 32-bit Linux kernel, kernel virtual address enable the kernel to
          use more than 1 GB (from 0xC0000000 until 0xFFFFFFFF).

  - Page table structure:
    - Moving to a 64-bit address space affects page table structure in x86.
      Because x86 uses a multi-level page table, the current 64-bit systems use
      a four-level page table. The full 64-bit nature of the virtual address
      space is not yet in use, only the bottom 48 bits is in use (bit 0 until
      bit 47).

  - Large page support:
    - Intel x86 allows for the use of multiple page sizes, not just the standard
      4 KB page. Specifically, the recent designs support 2 MB and even 1 GB
      pages in hardware. Thus, over time Linux kernel evolved to allow
      applications to utilize these huge pages (the term in Linux kernel).
    - When a process actively uses a large amount of memory, the process quickly
      fills up the TLB with translations. If those translations are for 4 KB
      pages, only a _small amount_ of the total memory can be accessed without
      inducing TLB misses. Recent research[B+13 reference in the book] shows
      that some applications spend 10% of their cycles servicing TLB misses.
    - Huge pages allow a process to access a large portion of memory without TLB
      misses by using _fewer slots_ in the TLB. One huge page's virtual page
      number (VPN) in the TLB can hold a lot of virtual addresses, so we will
      have more TLB hit just by having one VPN in the TLB.
    - Other benefits of huge pages is the shorter TLB miss path, which means
      that when TLB miss does occur, it is serviced more quickly (because
      there's only one page table?).
    - Linux kernel implement huge pages as on-demand allocation (either through
      the mmap() or shmget() calls) rather than making that as the default,
      which means the applications need to explicitly request the memory
      allocations with huge pages. In this way, most applications would not be
      affected (and continue using 4 KB pages), and only a few demanding
      applications that need to be changed to use these interfaces.
    - More recently, as the need for better TLB behavior is more common among
      many applications, Linux kernel added _transparent_ huge page support.
      When this feature is enabled, the operating system automatically looks
      for opportunity to allocate huge pages (usually 2 MB, but 1 GB on some
      systems) without requiring application modification.
    - The biggest trade off using huge pages is "internal fragmentation", which
      is a page that is large but sparsely used. This can fill memory with large
      but little (actual) used pages.
    - Swapping also does not work well with huge pages, sometimes greatly
      amplifying the amount of I/O a system does.

  - Page cache:
    - Linux kernel page cache is unified, keeping pages in memory from three
      primary sources in a "page cache hash table", allowing for quick lookup
      when the data is needed. These three primary sources are:
      - Memory-mapped files.
      - File data and metadata from devices (usually accessed by directing
        read() and write() system call to the file system).
      - Heap and stack pages that cover each process (sometimes called
        _anonymous memory_ because there's no named file underneath).
    - The page cache tracks if entries are clean (read but not modified) or
      dirty (modified). Dirty data is periodically written to the backing store
      (specific file for file data, or swap space for anonymous memory) by
      background threads (called pdflush), thus ensuring that modified data
      eventually is written back to persistent storage. This background activity
      either takes place after a certain time period or if we have too many
      pages that are considered dirty, this background activity is configurable.
    - The Linux kernel version of 2Q page replacement policy use 2 lists and
      dividing memory between them. When the page accessed for the first time,
      the page is placed on "the inactive list". When the page is re-referenced,
      the page is promoted to "the active list". When we need to replace the
      page, the candidate for replacement is taken from the inactive list. Linux
      kernel also periodically moves pages from the bottom of the active list to
      inactive list, keeping the active list to about two-thirds of the total
      page cache size.

  - Buffer overflow:
    - The idea of these attack is to find a bug in the target system which lets
      the attacker inject arbitrary data into the target's address space.
    - Such vulnerabilities sometime arise because the developer assumes that an
      input will not be overly long, and thus copies the input into a buffer.
      But because these input is long, it overflows the allocated buffer and
      _overwriting_ memory of the target.
    - Some malicious programmers can carefully craft the input that overflows
      the buffer so as to _inject_ their own code into the targeted system and
      allow them to take over the system (run arbitrary computations or gaining
      kernel access right a.k.a privilege escalation).
    - The first and simple _defense_ against buffer overflow is prevent
      execution of any code found within certain regions of an address space
      (like within the stack), basically like having execute permission.
      The NX (No-eXecute) bit in AMD and XD in intel x86 architecture prevent
      execution from any page which has these bit set in its corresponding page
      table entry (PTE). This approach prevents code injected by an attacker
      into the target's stack from being executed, and thus mitigates the
      previous problem.

  - Return Oriented Programming (ROP):
    - The observation behind ROP is that there are lots of bits of code within
      any program's address space, especially C programs that link with a lot of
      C library. Thus, an attacker can overwrite the stack such that
      _the return address_ in the currently executing function points to a
      desired malicious instruction (or series of instructions), followed by a
      return instruction.
    - By stringing together a large number of code (ensuring each return jumps
      to the next malicious code), the attacker can execute arbitrary code.
    - To _defend_ against ROP (and its earlier form, the return-to-libc attack),
      Linux and other systems add another defense mechanism known as address
      space layout randomization (ASLR). The idea of ASLR is that instead of
      placing the code, stack, and heap at fixed locations within the virtual
      address space, the operating system randomizes their placement, thus
      making it quite challenging to craft the intricate code sequence required
      to implement ROP attacks.
    - ASLR is a useful defense mechanism that the Linux kernel also implemented
      its own kernel address space layout randomization (KASLR).

  - Meltdown and Spectre
    - The general weakness exploited in Meltdown and Spectre is that the CPUs in
      modern systems perform all sort of "crazy" behind-the-scenes tricks to
      improve performance. One class of technique that lies at the core of the
      problem is called "speculative execution", in which the CPU _guesses_
      which instructions will soon be executed in the future, and start
      executing them _ahead of time_. If the guesses are correct, the program
      runs faster, and if not, the CPU undo their effects on architectural state
      (like registers) and tries again.
    - The problem  with "speculation execution" is that it tends to
      "leave traces" of its execution in various parts of the system, such as
      processor caches, branch predictors, etc. Such state can make vulnerable
      the contents of memory, even memory that we thought was protected by the
      memory management unit (MMU).
    - One way to increase the kernel protection was thus to remove as much of
      the kernel address space from each of user process and use a _separate_
      kernel page table for most of the kernel data (called kernel page table
      isolation or KPTI). Thus, instead of mapping the kernel's code and data
      structures into each process, only the barest minimum is kept therein.
      When a context switch happen (switching to kernel mode), a switch to the
      kernel page table is now needed.
    - Using KPTI improve security and avoids some attack vectors but at the cost
      of performance, as switching page table is costly.
